third = {
    4: "Expansion generally elicited stronger steering than rotation, but both responses plateaued at approximately 3–10 Hz and declined near 30 Hz. Matching temporal-frequency optima for two different flow fields are consistent with a shared early stage of elementary motion detection feeding both steering responses. The tuning is expressed in temporal frequency because stripe spacing and pattern speed jointly set how quickly contrast changes at each retinal position.",
    6: "The elementary motion detector is a local computation comparing signals from different visual positions over time. Its historical derivation described operations sufficient to explain behavior. Later fly experiments tested that computation and traced it to identified cells, T4 and T5 among them.",
    8: "Clark's random-bar experiments supported this correlation structure in fly turning, with a fitted delay filter peaking near 25 ms. That delay summarizes the combined effect of synaptic kinetics, membrane filtering and circuit connections, any of which can supply the temporal offset between the two inputs.",
    9: "The weak random-bar stimulus produced substantial turning unrelated to the stimulus, so the fit accounted for only a small fraction of the trial-by-trial variability. What it captured was the average directional interaction, with predicted and measured mean turning related approximately linearly.",
    10: "Changing wavelength changes the time between neighboring contrast events at a fixed speed. A detector can therefore respond differently to two patterns moving at the same velocity. The measured tuning curve reflects the whole pathway, with spatial pooling, adaptation and the dynamics of the motor response shaping it alongside the local detector.",
    11: "Contrast inversion changes the sign of interactions between neighboring signals in a correlation computation. Reverse-phi therefore probes the detector's operations, rather than simply testing whether the fly can track an identifiable object. At high velocities the turning becomes aligned with the displacement again, so the response depends on stimulus speed as well as on contrast sign.",
    12: "These results constrain how ON and OFF pathways can implement the correlation computation. Even with bright and dark events processed in separate pathways, the behavior requires effective sensitivity to opposite-polarity events, which could come from baseline activity, inhibition or another circuit operation.",
    13: "Shinomiya and colleagues reconstructed neurons and synapses with focused-ion-beam scanning electron microscopy. T4 dendrites occupy medulla layer M10, whereas T5 dendrites occupy lobula layer Lo1. Their axons converge in the lobula plate, so two anatomically separate input routes feed a common output neuropil.",
    14: "L1-associated processing favored light-edge motion, whereas L2-associated processing favored dark-edge motion. Both pathways retained overlapping sensitivity, and their light-evoked terminal calcium responses were similar at the relay stage, so the edge preferences are not simply a consequence of L1 and L2 responding to light with opposite signs.",
    16: "Haag and colleagues measured direction-dependent calcium responses in both pathways. Parallel organization means different input populations and different edge preferences, with the two pathways converging onto separate dendrites before their axons enter the lobula plate.",
    18: "T4 neurons responded most strongly near the receptive-field center and less strongly at neighboring positions. Because each location was tested individually, the map gives the response that every position contributes alone, the baseline against which a stimulus sequence can be judged for a nonlinear interaction.",
    19: "Subtracting the linear prediction from the measured response separated enhancement from suppression. Because only the order of the same positions changed between the two sequences, the directional difference is a nonlinear interaction at or before T4 output rather than a consequence of unequal brightness or a stronger central flash.",
    20: "A delayed excitatory interaction is consistent with a Reichardt-like enhancement mechanism, in which a delayed signal from one position combines multiplicatively with the signal arriving from its neighbor. The roughly 0.1 s optimum belongs to this calcium-imaging protocol and differs from the delay of about 25 ms fitted to fly turning behavior.",
    22: "The studies used different measurements and preparations, one imaging calcium responses and the other counting and locating synapses. Both found structured contributions around the center, and the displaced inputs are candidate sources of the spatial asymmetry that makes reversing a sequence change the combined response.",
    23: "The same classes of nonlinear operation can therefore occur in both ON and OFF pathways despite different input neurons and dendritic locations. T5 receives its inputs in the lobula, so the matching enhancement and suppression are an experimental result rather than a consequence of identical circuitry, and spacing mattered because T5's effective receptive-field organization differs from that of T4.",
    24: "Enhancement and suppression therefore form a common functional organization across the tested ON and OFF subtypes. The same arrangement, enhancement on one side of the receptive field and suppression on the other, coexists with different receptive-field sizes or temporal filters in different subtypes.",
    25: "Input position constrains which spatial signals can interact within T4, and the unequal distribution along the dendrite provides a physical substrate for unequal contributions from neighboring visual positions. Synapse number measures anatomical connectivity; the physiological effect of a contact also depends on release, receptor properties and the neuron's electrical state.",
    26: "The expanded reconstruction did not reproduce a consistent Mi1–Tm3 spatial offset proposed from an earlier volume. The weight of the directional asymmetry therefore falls on additional displaced input channels and their functional time courses.",
    28: "These spatial relationships resemble the differentiated input zones of T4 while using a different set of relay neurons. Central and displaced input classes can therefore deliver distinct information to different parts of the same T5 dendrite.",
    30: "Routes to T4 and T5 can contain different numbers of intervening neurons, and their effective timing also depends on membrane and synaptic dynamics. The spatially ordered inputs give the receiving neuron signals from different visual positions, and the delay between those signals can arise from route length and from synaptic properties, which is how the Reichardt algorithm and the connectome fit together.",
    31: "The published heat map reports RPKM, reads per kilobase of transcript per million mapped reads, on a 0–100 color scale. The assay pooled T4 and T5 and measured RNA, so each transcript reports expression in the combined population: it may occur in one class, in both or at different levels.",
    32: "Channel expression provides molecular candidates for active processing and calcium entry in T4/T5. An auxiliary subunit can change how a channel behaves without forming the pore that carries the ions, so the detected para subunits and calcium-channel transcripts point to several ways membrane voltage can be transformed into calcium signals.",
    33: "The authors proposed that active membrane conductances could contribute to delays and nonlinear interactions within motion detectors. Potassium-channel families differ in their gating properties, so the expression list offers several candidate mechanisms for how an earlier input influences the response to a later one.",
    34: "The transcriptome also detected vesicular GABA-transporter expression, a molecular hint of additional signaling. The physiological and pharmacological results support a primarily cholinergic output with an indirect inhibitory route, in which an intervening pathway supplies the inhibition instead of every T4/T5 terminal releasing an inhibitory transmitter directly.",
    35: "The voltage-to-calcium transformation therefore contributes to measured direction selectivity rather than simply copying membrane voltage. Calcium-based estimates of the circuit's selectivity can exceed voltage-based estimates, so the selectivity read out in calcium is sharper than that of the voltage inputs from which it derives.",
    36: "The authors compared published filtering and nonlinear-transform models with the measurements, and models containing temporal filtering and a nonlinear step captured how voltage maps onto calcium. Synaptic direction computation and the later voltage-to-calcium transformation are distinct contributions that can coexist within T4.",
    37: "Mauss and colleagues labeled T4/T5 terminals together with recorded LPTCs, relating the input population to its downstream targets. Pooling changes the spatial scale of the signal: a local detector evaluates motion over a restricted part of the visual field, whereas a tangential cell's response reflects a broad distribution of motion across the eye.",
    39: "Picrotoxin at 25 µM reduced responses to pressure-applied glutamate as well as GABA, so the inhibitory conductance in the indirect route could be GABAergic, glutamatergic or both. Different sensitivities to the cholinergic antagonists are consistent with different receptor populations on the direct and indirect routes.",
    40: "Forward walking speed remained comparable across the tested genotypes. The behavioral deficit therefore was not explained by an inability to walk. T4/T5 are necessary for these directional responses, and because the same silencing removed both ordinary and illusory turning, the illusion draws on the same early motion circuit as ordinary stabilizing turns.",
    41: "The neuronal response therefore partially inverted with the illusion rather than reproducing the ordinary response with its sign flipped at every stage. Direct voltage recordings distinguish active hyperpolarization from a response near baseline, which a calcium signal near zero cannot do. The agreement between neural and behavioral direction supports an early sensory contribution to the illusory turn.",
    42: "The two-quadrant model included a baseline luminance contribution, which allows effective opposite-polarity influences without multiplying every ON and OFF pair directly. Clark's polarity tests and Leonhardt's dissociated stimuli both require sensitivity beyond two isolated, perfectly rectified streams, and anatomical ON/OFF separation remains compatible with functional influence from both contrast signs.",
    43: "Spatial and temporal tuning also differed between the initial 0.5-s response and the later 1–4-s response, so a measurement averaged over the whole stimulus would hide the dynamics. Because the external motion never reversed, the change in turning direction arises from processing within the fly, through downstream dynamics or adaptation.",
    44: "The results support a transformation downstream of the sampled lobula plate signals. Silencing establishes that detector output is required for both the early and the late turn, whereas the imaging places the sign change beyond the detector: preferred-direction signals persisted while the behavior inverted.",
    45: "The finding supports visual-experience-dependent maturation of sensorimotor responses, with dark rearing preserving more juvenile-like dynamics than adult age alone. The same article found different age dependence in Drosophila yakuba, which retained more stable anti-directional responses over the examined early adult interval, so the maturation of these dynamics differs between closely related fly species.",
}

